#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1b10a6-d28a-5db7-814c-c25882d15ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288139} {:answer/id #uuid "5ecf0e48-0097-5dda-a253-b2d64f4f587a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288694} {:answer/id #uuid "8d71ca93-fb70-58aa-ab40-f7a91b2211f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288978} {:answer/id #uuid "8f066df5-2d87-5e05-95fd-14e9b77fb3ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288990} {:answer/id #uuid "e3aa48ff-1d31-52e7-9948-ea52afba72ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f066df5-2d87-5e05-95fd-14e9b77fb3ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288990} :answer-field/id #uuid "299710cf-4029-522c-adb1-4b2c1979ed84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b73efa01-82ab-533b-98f8-7ad100b1bc59" :question/math-academy-id "q-22615" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le \\pi ,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\sqrt{3}$\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}$\n- The global minimum value is $0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =0+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}≈3.8 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )=\\pi ≈3.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](images/53/539dc4ccd2f1b20f521ae0f70bb5d95b8c5f9d1a006ff102baf43ac13ce19660.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54d8754e-929a-5793-91c1-8400263bfef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288617} {:answer/id #uuid "8b98664e-7579-5263-9822-e8d6cc4311dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288964} {:answer/id #uuid "9f536586-0671-5a5a-89aa-93928ef4966d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289099} {:answer/id #uuid "cc115c37-1333-5a52-8961-f8946c3603ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289388} {:answer/id #uuid "fd461939-6dd2-5f9c-ab7d-59c1cd8be4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b98664e-7579-5263-9822-e8d6cc4311dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288964} :answer-field/id #uuid "1076ef24-fac9-5cde-b155-83bc1ecb5aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52b51aa0-5a9a-5119-8333-c08e35e82d61" :question/math-academy-id "q-22754" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+2},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- $x=1$ is a local minimum\n- $x=2$ is a local maximum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2x+2)-(x-1)\\cdot (2x-2)}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+2-2{x}^{2}+4x-2}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ and $x=2$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{-(-1)^{2}+2(-1)}{((-1)^{2}-2(-1)+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{-(1)^{2}+2(1)}{((1)^{2}-2(1)+2)^{2}}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{-(3)^{2}+2(3)}{((3)^{2}-2(3)+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066b3f2a-935a-58e3-b45b-5ef8105ae921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288083} {:answer/id #uuid "4bddd408-461f-59df-971c-0fbf3e710d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288556} {:answer/id #uuid "5fda4d33-676b-57f0-8b2a-aebb545ffce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288702} {:answer/id #uuid "638e801a-59a0-5f14-bea4-c312ba4e168a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288730} {:answer/id #uuid "71f6b8a1-afac-5574-9400-c0d6dd93e7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71f6b8a1-afac-5574-9400-c0d6dd93e7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288826} :answer-field/id #uuid "d43b6123-f31f-5681-9bc2-f17e4c41ee8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "abc739c6-0758-56d6-9f78-098fd3207a39" :question/math-academy-id "q-2455" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=3x-{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)>0$\n- $x=-1$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=3x-{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3-3{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(1-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3(1-x)(1+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm 1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-6x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =6>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=-1,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45594262-3efe-57c7-b5b9-bfdbc6564c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290917} {:answer/id #uuid "7620126f-9b05-57e1-83c0-abc96aed6552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291136} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb07632-b195-5870-b2a7-66c1d64cb5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291169} {:answer/id #uuid "a844a83e-0aac-539f-89a3-bca1ef965168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291368} {:answer/id #uuid "fba97015-0947-55e9-a31d-e68b17721999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eb07632-b195-5870-b2a7-66c1d64cb5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291169} :answer-field/id #uuid "966e70db-9154-5f74-a132-3c4d2bdeac39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bea0a781-9898-5e25-a447-5b87446089a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-2690" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x+3,$ on the closed interval $-4\\le x\\le -2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $3$\n- The global minimum value is $1$\n- The global minimum value is $-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+12x+9 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+4x+3) \\\\  & =3(x+1)(x+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n3(x+1)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\\,-1.\n$$\n\nSince $x=-1$ does not belong to the interval $[-4,-2]$, the critical points are $x=-3$ and the endpoints $x=-4,-2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =(-4)^{3}+6(-4)^{2}+9(-4)+3=-1 \\\\ f(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+6(-3)^{2}+9(-3)+3=3 \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2)^{3}+6(-2)^{2}+9(-2)+3=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-3)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-4)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](images/fa/fa85119166ca325f9925ddd03a578fb5daae463c59c3b7b9f7ac0f6464a30241.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8f7ef5-274c-5a18-bf40-dbd12cbd1f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290684} {:answer/id #uuid "4931603f-83bf-5071-a3bd-c0b45754444b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290935} {:answer/id #uuid "b24ab148-c47f-5ea1-b796-e3809a66f34a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291410} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f9769d-7490-55c3-9765-366a8800bb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291463} {:answer/id #uuid "c6025f92-8fc3-59aa-969a-c9a5d636c25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4931603f-83bf-5071-a3bd-c0b45754444b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290935} :answer-field/id #uuid "e349e4b2-4399-55f4-ab33-3c8c124c76c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e48a4a75-01e8-5c53-b3a8-5931680804d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-2691" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+4,$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $4$\n- The global maximum value is $2$\n- The global minimum value is $0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-6x \\\\  & =2x(2{x}^{2}-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2x(2{x}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}$ do not belong to the interval $[-1,1]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{4}-3(-1)^{2}+4=2 \\\\ f(0) & =(0)^{4}-3(0)^{2}+4=4 \\\\ f(1) & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}+4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=4,$ so the global maximum is $4.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-1)=f(1)=2,$ so the global minimum is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n![](images/a7/a7141a342d971d5b520b2f7f07f8fdb1c01ddca18a983fb0c83102a5bd4aee8e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d7bb9f1-b157-599d-93ed-6607f6670caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295620} {:answer/id #uuid "57f33323-a574-5d3a-9709-4eb09849a56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295774} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d54f7c-6d02-5810-afd3-16ba3cfaf37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296154} {:answer/id #uuid "bba10e7d-2cc2-5341-a2f3-b9fb123d6f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296250} {:answer/id #uuid "e870b774-8e7e-5746-ab82-10ba9e3d3195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d7bb9f1-b157-599d-93ed-6607f6670caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295620} :answer-field/id #uuid "543e005e-a445-5e44-847a-061f1c3bcb23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c7808ae-1f8f-5217-b026-8ee08973156b" :question/math-academy-id "q-28981" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=(0.08)^{x}?$\n\nI. Its range is $y>0$\n\nII. It is an increasing function\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(0.08)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4e3b5119c92d02bbceb786fefd238182c7c84ca0f4abc85a19f2df86a6ba267.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(0.08)}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement II is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a25b1a2-e20d-5c53-8a13-6c9232a5dc7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298008} {:answer/id #uuid "752fe78a-fefa-52a6-a225-a83083b62369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298058} {:answer/id #uuid "757d1ce4-f1bf-5fd0-804f-f3630bd25740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298061} {:answer/id #uuid "f385759e-cbba-56ac-bed7-1e81bce59c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298583} {:answer/id #uuid "f81ece97-48d2-5de9-a082-e3eacc213705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "757d1ce4-f1bf-5fd0-804f-f3630bd25740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298061} :answer-field/id #uuid "72f65d6e-c5b7-516f-bba7-11721b3aeb79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a35d343-f492-5478-a8b0-3c966904f7aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-31907" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $Ax=0$ has an infinite number of solutions" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237c21a2-58d9-5482-a65d-954647423b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302680} {:answer/id #uuid "ab460f1b-11f6-526a-9b16-4af30d826eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303510} {:answer/id #uuid "b36e18e5-31bb-5dbf-abee-67b6b6b12210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303570} {:answer/id #uuid "baef488a-08fa-557d-97ca-8a58d1d3de1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303611} {:answer/id #uuid "fb50f52d-0998-5637-b066-ecf0170309a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab460f1b-11f6-526a-9b16-4af30d826eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303510} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a682bf-a032-50ac-86b9-905f1dfd7634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0df5f53a-0779-5ce7-90dc-03bd51afb4c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332674" :question/problem "Let $f(t)$ be a piecewise function defined on $[0,\\infty )$ with exponential order $a,$ and consider its Laplace transform \n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)},\\,s>a.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $f(t)$ contains a discontinuity for some value of $t>0,$ we have $F(s)=0$ for all $s>a.$\n- $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable on $s>a.$\n- The Laplace transform converts a non-smooth $f(t)$ into a smooth function of $s.$" :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Smoothness has no bearing on whether the Laplace transform vanishes.\nA function of exponential order always has a well-defined Laplace transform for $s>a$, and there is no reason for that transform to be identically zero.\n\n- Statement II is true. The smoothness theorem states that for $f(t)$ of exponential order, the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f}(s)\n$$\n\nis infinitely differentiable at every point in its region of convergence, and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}(s).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The Laplace transform $F(s)$ of a function $f(t)$ is always infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence, even if $f(t)$ is non-smooth.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27327ea8-ce27-5b3e-80d1-a3469c81de6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305517} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbb919f-f596-5390-a734-15dd30acfa32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305771} {:answer/id #uuid "61010ec6-8cd1-5dad-bf8d-1dcd8add9646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306086} {:answer/id #uuid "925f80f4-2747-5d08-906a-4fe00980b955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306535} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf6c9e5-ba76-5da5-a02b-bb30d8c02912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61010ec6-8cd1-5dad-bf8d-1dcd8add9646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306086} :answer-field/id #uuid "e69407ce-3a88-501e-b535-736d132d0233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86209978-f558-5e50-81ba-e94e00e9e46b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333340" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=3,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{7}{3},$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n- The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|>1.$\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=3,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{7}{3}y,\\,y(0)=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{7}{3}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{3}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)((1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =3{(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is false.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{7}{3}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{7}{3}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.9$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b4d65d-477c-5947-941e-6499f2f1f6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305358} {:answer/id #uuid "297268fa-ba76-5d31-ac32-702aa4472c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305543} {:answer/id #uuid "9fdd7c6f-c0fd-5b8a-927d-2868562038b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306651} {:answer/id #uuid "c63d1277-60bd-5595-aed1-3ed0f74eca7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306995} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe5c313-0094-5138-8ac3-e920c477f56a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffe5c313-0094-5138-8ac3-e920c477f56a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307514} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3b9773c-0989-5519-98b6-189f795e50fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6837c06-642a-5ef0-97aa-16a1aa0e164f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333344" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=5,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2},$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n- The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx|<1.$\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.3.$\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=5,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}y,\\,y(0)=5.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)((1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =5{(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{3}{2}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{3}{2}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=1$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c0c11d-4619-5127-9b7c-ea5346e63bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305360} {:answer/id #uuid "29d71e82-f14a-5349-b979-2127dc4d7a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305551} {:answer/id #uuid "59716f29-4d1c-5f2f-a563-b825e498a9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306008} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1a4714-e2a5-597c-86a1-7828bc413720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307199} {:answer/id #uuid "f36da0b7-eaa6-5894-9012-f09ed62cacc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59716f29-4d1c-5f2f-a563-b825e498a9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306008} :answer-field/id #uuid "552aeabf-c688-5ebe-bc78-78c0aa5c4a04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bf24239-c81d-5a71-bce2-db470e9ad2d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333347" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{9}{4},$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n}.$\n- The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1.$\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.6.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{9}{4}y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{9}{4}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{9}{4}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)((1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{9}{4}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{9}{4}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.6$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516210e5-fc50-5d1d-9694-0433da9e13e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305941} {:answer/id #uuid "890c1b2d-0e4b-598a-b9e7-d4af9496064c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306463} {:answer/id #uuid "a84ba563-15db-5c7f-8709-ec4fd2a7a8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306739} {:answer/id #uuid "acdd1596-8871-5fd0-bdb6-a2a2a071bf71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306781} {:answer/id #uuid "daa8c6e2-2d17-5c37-ba66-430820547f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acdd1596-8871-5fd0-bdb6-a2a2a071bf71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306781} :answer-field/id #uuid "f49d813f-7742-5438-9015-e19e3fc2cca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4be1bdfb-f8fa-566e-b0ec-d9b21f97e58a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333398" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=6,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{8}{3},$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n- The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1.$\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.25.$\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.8.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=6,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{3}y,\\,y(0)=6.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{8}{3}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{8}{3}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)((1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =6{(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{8}{3}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{8}{3}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.25$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.8$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e09d670-2778-5b5e-9470-0dca29e54749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306052} {:answer/id #uuid "81e6c1d2-c4fb-536e-a51c-337f3b1a93d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306393} {:answer/id #uuid "94f703fe-1822-5dbe-a433-9666dbdfb2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306555} {:answer/id #uuid "9abc2760-cde2-525a-829b-f13794560f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306596} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d745ad-b635-595e-b60a-6f6d573e4328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9d745ad-b635-595e-b60a-6f6d573e4328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307167} :answer-field/id #uuid "32fa52cb-801f-5620-a1c1-57349a96d831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed443d52-d157-5213-99c2-1ffa8b393121" :question/math-academy-id "q-333546" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =6,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1-6Δx}.$\n- The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.10.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-6y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-6{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+6{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+6Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}.\n$$\n\nThis shows that the statement I is false.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(6Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+6Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.10.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165ce28b-eebe-5779-8e38-224ac1b86d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305350} {:answer/id #uuid "35e13847-002e-5dad-b9ab-d6458b9715c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305670} {:answer/id #uuid "4741fe5d-ca6e-53b1-b16d-0917dc2338a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305843} {:answer/id #uuid "6158af16-7a83-5bcc-885b-a6898d809d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306089} {:answer/id #uuid "77deb2de-27d2-54cd-bb15-99cd7ce2f936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35e13847-002e-5dad-b9ab-d6458b9715c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305670} :answer-field/id #uuid "44e5cfd2-d2ac-5d16-afe6-096d974c3e07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a2ae136-3885-5a0c-a306-c58f92aeffc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333572" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =5,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$\n- The stability region is $|\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-5{y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-5Δx|}{|2+5Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-5Δx| & <|2+5Δx| \\\\ (2-5Δx)^{2} & <(2+5Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} & <4+20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -20Δx & <20Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=1,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d189f8b-dc17-5ea2-acc4-3f8618e54aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305265} {:answer/id #uuid "6b01b926-e6c3-5eca-bf5c-7f481c0a99d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306174} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc5e198-3b8b-5327-be00-39689b100fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306912} {:answer/id #uuid "c28afe36-533d-537a-83ad-e1f16a2fb71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306965} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f12a7f-e3d4-53e1-97a7-b6941b9ed996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d189f8b-dc17-5ea2-acc4-3f8618e54aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305265} :answer-field/id #uuid "db8d2cbd-77c6-55da-828e-c4e6af562c35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66509616-e823-5ab6-81d6-06eb6bc735de" :question/math-academy-id "q-333574" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =2,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(2Δx)=\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}.$\n- The stability region is $|2{(Δx-1)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=2.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-2{y}_{n}+(-2{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(2Δx)=\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|1-Δx|}{|1+Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |1-Δx| & <|1+Δx| \\\\ (1-Δx)^{2} & <(1+Δx)^{2} \\\\ 1-2Δx+(Δx)^{2} & <1+2Δx+(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -2Δx & <2Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=2,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c0d778c-628a-51d3-b44b-f0c445013b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305409} {:answer/id #uuid "50b6b47e-04c9-5660-b676-2264bccf75d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305934} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0addf4-957c-51a8-8191-363fbb1c40e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306481} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7558c3-1504-5268-bba9-b3f3adbfcdfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306778} {:answer/id #uuid "f92ef6ae-5dea-5e21-8d1c-fdd8cde1c7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f92ef6ae-5dea-5e21-8d1c-fdd8cde1c7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307459} :answer-field/id #uuid "0393e4b6-9b3f-5fb2-8b6d-4a5eeb1202c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55adb6e6-36a2-5272-9189-7161d38a92cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-333578" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =7,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1+7Δx/2}{1-7Δx/2}.$\n- The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-7{y}_{n}+(-7{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(7Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-7Δx|}{|2+7Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-7Δx| & <|2+7Δx| \\\\ (2-7Δx)^{2} & <(2+7Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} & <4+28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -28Δx & <28Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}]}
